Arriving at Mars was the hardest part. Mangalyaan had to slow down enough for Mars's [[gravity|gravity]] to catch it. If the main engine failed, it would sail straight past and be lost forever.

That engine had been switched off for about 300 days. Two days before arrival, ISRO fired it for just four seconds as a test. It worked. On 24 September, it burned for about 24 minutes, and Mars captured the spacecraft. Radio signals took about 12 minutes to reach Earth, so the team in Bengaluru waited without knowing until the news arrived. Prime Minister Narendra Modi watched from the control centre.

{{story:vikram-sarabhai}}

Mercury's spin is barely tilted, so near its poles the Sun always sits low on the horizon. The floors of some deep craters there never see sunlight at all. They stay colder than −170 °C, all the time.

Radar from Earth first spotted bright patches in these craters in 1991. Then NASA's MESSENGER spacecraft, which orbited Mercury from 2011 to 2015, confirmed they hold [[@moon-water|water ice]], possibly delivered by [[@comets|comets]] long ago. It's a strange thought: ice on the planet nearest the Sun.

{{story:calendars}}

King cobras hunt other snakes, including rat snakes, kraits, pythons and even other venomous [[species|species]]. They can follow a scent trail with their flicking forked tongue and swallow prey longer than a metre.

Because snakes are slippery and hard to catch, king cobras don't eat often. After a big meal, one may go weeks without food. Their [[@snakes-venom|venom]] acts mainly on the nerves, stopping prey from moving or breathing.

{{story:snakes-venom}}

About 2,600 years ago, the Greek thinker Thales of Miletus is said to have noticed something odd. He saw that [[amber|amber]] rubbed with fur could pick up feathers and bits of straw. The Greek word for amber was *elektron*, and that is where our words "electron" and "electricity" come from.

[[@amber-fossils|Amber]] is [[fossil|fossil]] tree resin, and some pieces have insects trapped inside from millions of years ago. For centuries, rubbed amber was a curiosity. Only in the 1600s and 1700s did European scientists start building machines that made static on purpose, rubbing spinning glass globes and collecting the charge in jars.

{{story:lightning}}

Warhol learned that he could make art the way [[@advertising|advertising]] made posters. In silkscreen printing, an image is put onto a fine mesh stretched over a frame. Ink is pushed through the mesh onto canvas, in a way similar to [[@block-printing|block printing]] or stencilling. He could print the same photo dozens of times.

His studio, covered in silver foil and paint, was full of assistants, musicians, actors and visitors. Warhol made paintings, prints, films and even a magazine there. Repeating images, he once suggested, can make them feel both more powerful and more empty.

{{story:advertising}}

For years, people thought ancient tattoos were only dots and lines. Then, in 2018, scientists at the British Museum used [[infrared|infrared]] light to look at two [[@mummies|mummies]] from Gebelein in Egypt, about 5,000 years old. A man had a wild bull and a sheep tattooed on his arm. A woman had S-shaped marks on her shoulder.

These are the oldest picture tattoos known. In later [[@ancient-egypt|ancient Egypt]], several female mummies carry patterns of dots and lines, and one from about 3,000 years ago has more than 30 tattoos, including eyes, flowers and cows.

{{story:mummies}}

Many kolams start with a grid of dots, called *pulli*. The artist then draws lines that loop and curl around the dots. In one style, the *sikku* kolam, a single continuous line weaves around every dot and ends where it began, without ever crossing a dot.

Kolams use mirror and rotating [[@symmetry|symmetry]], and many repeat in tidy patterns. In the 1970s and 1980s, the computer scientist Gift Siromoney and his colleagues at Madras Christian College in Chennai studied kolams as "picture languages", working out rules that could generate them. Kolam artists, most of them women with no formal maths training, had been following such rules by memory for generations.

{{story:symmetry}}

In 1927, B. R. [[@ambedkar|Ambedkar]] led thousands of people to the Chavdar Tale, a public water tank in Mahad, Maharashtra. People of so-called "untouchable" castes were forbidden to drink from it. They drank anyway, claiming a right that was theirs by law. The Mahad Satyagraha became a landmark in the fight against caste discrimination.

In 1954, the US Supreme Court ruled in Brown v. Board of Education that separate schools for Black and white children were unequal and against the Constitution. In 1960, six-year-old [[@usa-ruby-bridges|Ruby Bridges]] became the first Black child at William Frantz Elementary School in New Orleans. Angry crowds shouted at her, and marshals walked her to class. The [[@civil-rights|civil rights movement]] that followed changed American law.

{{story:civil-rights}}

In 1863, Dunant and four other men in Geneva set up a committee to answer those questions. It became the ICRC. The next year, 12 countries signed the first Geneva Convention. It said wounded soldiers must be cared for, whatever side they were on, and that medical workers wearing the red cross must not be attacked.

More conventions followed, the latest in 1949, protecting prisoners of war and civilians. Today every country in the world has signed the 1949 Geneva Conventions. They are a big part of international [[@human-rights|human rights]] law.

{{story:nobel-peace}}

In the 1970s, rural women told her their streams were drying up, food was scarce, and they had to walk further each day for firewood. Forests had been cut for farms and plantations of tea and [[@coffee|coffee]].

Maathai saw that one action could tackle all these problems. Trees protect the soil from rain and wind. They help the ground hold water, and they make food and fuel, using sunlight through [[@photosynthesis|photosynthesis]]. So she began planting.

{{story:soil-made}}

Pi Day has another link. [[@einstein|Albert Einstein]], the physicist who changed how we think about time and space, was born on 14 March 1879. Many people celebrate his birthday on Pi Day too.

By chance, the British physicist Stephen Hawking died on 14 March 2018. Fans of science now remember both scientists on the same day.

{{story:einstein}}

Modellers sculpt each character on a computer, out of a mesh of thousands of tiny flat shapes. Next, riggers give the model a hidden skeleton, with hundreds of controls for the eyebrows, lips, fingers and tail. Pulling one control might make a character smile.

Other artists paint the surfaces. Is the skin shiny or rough? Is the shirt made of wool or plastic? These choices change how light will bounce off later. Every chair, tree and cloud in the background needs the same treatment.

{{story:pixels}}

Each team is owned by a company or businesspeople and represents a city. Players are bought at an auction, where teams bid against each other with fixed [[@budgets|budgets]]. Watching a young player from a small town go for crores of rupees has become a television event in itself.

An IPL team can field up to four overseas players at a time. Indian youngsters share a dressing room with world champions from other countries, which many say has made them better and braver.

{{story:streaming}}

For centuries, marbles were made from clay, stone or agate. In the 1800s, German glassmakers learned to make glass marbles in bulk, twisting coloured glass inside clear balls. In the early 1900s, factories in the USA built machines that could turn out millions.

Making a glass marble starts like any [[@glass-made|glass]]: sand, soda and lime melted together. A blob of molten glass, often streaked with colour, is cut and rolled between turning rollers until it cools into a perfect sphere.

{{story:glass-made}}

In his early films, Rajinikanth often played villains. Audiences loved his speed and swagger so much that he was soon cast as the hero. He became famous for tiny tricks done at lightning pace: flipping sunglasses onto his face, tossing a coin, or swinging his jacket over his shoulder.

Fans call it "style", and they cheer every move. Films like *Baashha* (1995) and *Muthu* (1995) made him a giant across South India. *Enthiran* (2010), in which he played both a scientist and a robot, used some of the most ambitious [[@special-effects|special effects]] Indian cinema had seen at the time.

{{story:japan-culture}}

In a car, your [[@ears-hear|inner ear]] feels the swaying. If you look out of the front window, your eyes see the road curving too, and everything matches. But if you look down at a book, your eyes see a still page. Your brain gets two different stories.

One old idea suggests the body treats this confusion like a sign of poisoning, since some poisons can scramble the senses. Vomiting would get rid of the poison. It's a neat explanation, but scientists still argue about it.

{{story:vr-ar}}

Nobel tried mixing nitroglycerine with many materials, including sawdust, paper and charcoal. The best was kieselguhr, a powder dug from the ground in Germany. It is made of the glassy shells of diatoms, single-celled [[algae|algae]] too small to see. Each shell is full of tiny holes, so the powder soaks up nearly three times its own weight of liquid and still feels dry.

The result could be rolled into sticks, dropped and even burned without exploding. It needed a blasting cap, a small charge set off by a fuse, to detonate.

{{story:tunnels}}

India's first [[telegraph|telegraph]] line opened in 1851, from Calcutta (now Kolkata) to Diamond Harbour. Within a few years, wires linked Calcutta, Bombay, Madras and Agra. During the revolt of 1857, telegraph clerks in Delhi sent warnings up the line to British stations in the Punjab. A British official later claimed the telegraph had saved British rule.

For more than a century, a telegram was how urgent news arrived: a new job, a birth, a death. Families often dreaded the knock of the telegram messenger. People paid by the word, so messages were short: "ARRIVING FRIDAY STOP". Telegrams travelled partly through the [[@post-office|post office]] network. India's service finally closed on 15 July 2013.

{{story:post-office}}

In 1968–69, in Jaipur, an orthopaedic [[surgeon|surgeon]] named Dr P.K. Sethi and a craftsman named Ram Chandra Sharma designed a new kind of artificial foot. Western prosthetic feet of the time were stiff and costly, and didn't suit people who walked barefoot, squatted or sat cross-legged on the floor.

The Jaipur Foot is made of rubber and wood, looks like a real foot, and can bend and twist. It lets people walk in fields, climb trees and work in paddy fields. A charity in [[@jaipur|Jaipur]], Bhagwan Mahaveer Viklang Sahayata Samiti, fits it free of charge. It has helped more than a million people, in India and at fitting camps in many countries of Asia and Africa.

{{story:paralympics}}

About 1.7 million years ago, a new style appeared in Africa: the hand axe. It is shaped like a teardrop, sharp all round, and fits neatly in the palm. This style, called Acheulean, spread across Africa, Europe and Asia and lasted for more than a million years. It may be the longest-used tool design in history.

Much later, people made microliths, tiny stone blades only a centimetre or two long. Several could be stuck into a wooden handle with resin to make a saw or a barbed arrow. Microliths have been found at many sites in India, including around the Bhimbetka rock shelters in Madhya Pradesh.

{{story:human-evolution}}

In the Diwan-i-Aam, the Hall of Public Audience, the emperor sat on a marble throne under a [[canopy|canopy]] and listened to ordinary people's requests and complaints. In the Diwan-i-Khas, the Hall of Private Audience, he met nobles and foreign visitors.

The Diwan-i-Khas once held the Peacock Throne, made of gold and covered in jewels, with two peacocks at its back. A Persian verse is carved in the hall. Roughly translated, it says that if there is a paradise on Earth, it is this, it is this, it is this. A stream called the Nahr-i-Bihisht, the Stream of Paradise, once flowed through the palace rooms, cooling them in summer.

{{story:revolt-1857}}

In the sequel, Through the Looking-Glass (1871), Alice climbs through a mirror into a back-to-front world. There she finds the [[@poetry|poem]] Jabberwocky, full of [[@words-made-up|invented words]] like "slithy", "brillig" and "frabjous". Humpty Dumpty explains that some of them are two words packed into one, like a suitcase.

Two of Carroll's inventions, "chortle" and "galumph", became real English words found in ordinary [[@dictionaries|dictionaries]]. The mirror world also plays games with reflection. Alice has to walk away from a place to reach it, and has to hold a book up to a [[@mirrors|mirror]] to read it.

{{story:mirrors}}

Raja Ram Mohan Roy, born in 1772, read Sanskrit, Persian, Arabic and English. He campaigned against sati, the practice of a widow dying on her husband's funeral pyre, which the British banned in 1829. In 1828 he founded the Brahmo Sabha, later the Brahmo Samaj, which worshipped one God without idols.

[[@ishwar-chandra|Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar]], a Sanskrit scholar, fought for widows to be allowed to remarry. He also pushed for girls' schools and simplified the Bengali alphabet. His primer, Barnaparichay, is still used.

{{story:jagadish-bose}}

In 1922, Epperson sold his frozen lollies at Neptune Beach, an amusement park across the bay from San Francisco. They were a hit. In 1924 he got a patent for "frozen confectionery" on a stick.

He first called them "Epsicles", joining his name and "icicle". His children called them "Pop's 'sicles", because their dad made them. That name stuck. In 1925, he sold the rights to a company in New York, and Popsicle became a famous brand.

{{story:body-brain-freeze}}
